WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.541
Python applications often sit behind frameworks such as Flask, Django, and FastAPI.

00:00:05.541 --> 00:00:12.723
Those frameworks receive request bodies, query strings, path parameters, headers, cookies, form fields, and uploaded files.

00:00:12.723 --> 00:00:18.263
Each of those values can be controlled or influenced by a client.

00:00:18.263 --> 00:00:27.087
The application should validate what the server receives, not what the browser or API documentation suggested a client should send.

00:00:27.087 --> 00:00:36.322
JSON payloads are common in Python APIs, but JSON parsing only tells the application that the payload is syntactically valid JSON.

00:00:36.322 --> 00:00:48.429
It does not prove that required fields exist, that values have safe lengths, that nested objects match expectations, or that the data is authorized for the current user.

00:00:48.429 --> 00:00:52.328
Query strings and headers have the same issue.

00:00:52.328 --> 00:00:56.637
They may parse correctly while still violating application rules.

00:00:56.637 --> 00:01:00.536
Python programs also process input outside web frameworks.

00:01:00.536 --> 00:01:10.591
Command-line arguments, environment variables, configuration files, YAML, XML, CSV, database records, message queues, and scheduled job payloads all carry data across trust boundaries.

00:01:10.591 --> 00:01:23.519
A batch job or internal admin script may feel safer than a public endpoint, but it can still process malformed imports, stale records, or data from a compromised upstream system.

00:01:23.519 --> 00:01:27.829
A practical validation strategy begins by inventorying input sources.

00:01:27.829 --> 00:01:41.578
For each source, ask who controls the data, how it is encoded, how it is parsed, what structure is expected, what business rule applies, and where the value will be used next.

00:01:41.578 --> 00:01:49.991
That map helps the team place validation close to the boundary and avoid treating internal plumbing as automatically trustworthy.
